   "content": "### Detailed Explanation of B-Tree Insertion, Splitting, and Rebalancing\n\nA B-tree is a self-balancing tree data structure that maintains sorted data and allows searches, sequential access, insertions, and deletions in logarithmic time. Unlike binary search trees, B-trees are designed to work well with storage systems that read and write relatively large blocks of data, such as disk drives. They maintain balance by ensuring that all leaves are at the same depth and that every internal node has a number of children within a specific range defined by the tree's order, $m$.\n\n#### 1. The Structure and Constraints\nA B-tree of order $m$ (often denoted as $B_m$) has the following properties:\n*   Every node has at most $m$ children.\n*   Every non-root internal node has at least $\\lceil m/2 \\rceil$ children.\n*   The root has at least 2 children if it is not a leaf.\n*   All leaves appear in the same level.\n*   A non-leaf node with $k$ children contains $k-1$ keys.\n\nWhen inserting a new key, the algorithm must ensure these constraints are not violated. If",
